EXT. CITY HALL PLAZA - DAY

A climate march, mid-afternoon. Banners. Drums. A hundred people sitting in a slow circle on the stone. Overhead, a sky the colour of old dishwater.

A kid in a polar bear costume walks a cardboard iceberg through the crowd. A woman with a stroller hands out oat milk. A dog in a tiny "Fossil Free" vest sniffs the leg of a bench.

On the bench: Terrence Bell, 40s, rumpled trench coat, a protest sign under his arm that says nothing at all. Blank cardboard.

A man sits down beside him. Gordon Achebe, 50s, expensive jacket pretending to be cheap. He holds a thermos.

Neither looks at the other.

GORDON
Nice turnout.

TERRENCE
Good cover.

GORDON
That's what it's for.

Terrence tilts the sign so the back is visible. A taped floor plan of the building behind them. Gordon glances, looks away.

GORDON (CONT'D)
You added the east stairwell.

TERRENCE
It's the only one without a sensor.

GORDON
I heard they installed one last week.

TERRENCE
You heard wrong. He lied to you.

GORDON
Who?

TERRENCE
The contractor.

A drum line starts up. The crowd chants something about carbon. A child shrieks with delight.

GORDON
He's my cousin.

TERRENCE
Then he lied to you. Family does.

A pause. Gordon unscrews the thermos. Pours. Offers a cup. Terrence takes it.

GORDON
It's tea.

TERRENCE
I know. I can smell the cardamom.

GORDON
My wife's.

TERRENCE
She know what you're doing?

GORDON
She thinks I'm at a rally.

TERRENCE
You are at a rally.

GORDON
Fair.

The polar bear kid walks by. Terrence nods at him. The kid nods back, very seriously.

TERRENCE
You ever think about how many people here actually believe it?

GORDON
All of them.

TERRENCE
All of them.

GORDON
That's why they sit like that. It's a thing you can only do if you believe.

TERRENCE
And us?

GORDON
We believe in a safe in a basement on the fourth floor.

Terrence laughs, quietly, into the cup.

The dog trots over, sits at their feet, looks up. Gordon breaks a biscuit from his pocket in half. Gives half to the dog. Eats the other.

GORDON (CONT'D)
Nine o'clock.

TERRENCE
Nine.

GORDON
Mayor's dinner. Everyone with a badge will be at it.

TERRENCE
And the protesters?

GORDON
Still sitting. They'll sit until midnight.

TERRENCE
Good people.

GORDON
Good enough to be useful.

Terrence says nothing. He looks at the circle of bodies on the stone. He looks at his blank sign.

This is the last time they will speak before the building.

TERRENCE
You'll take the stairwell.

GORDON
You'll take the elevator.

TERRENCE
It's loud.

GORDON
That's the point.

A woman with a megaphone climbs onto a plinth. The crowd quiets. She begins to read names of drowned towns.

They listen. Both of them. Longer than they planned to.

Gordon stands. Screws the lid on the thermos. Pats Terrence's shoulder, once, a stranger's courtesy.

GORDON (CONT'D)
Don't be late.

TERRENCE
I'm never late.

Gordon walks into the crowd. A teenager hands him a flyer. He takes it. Folds it. Pockets it.

Terrence stays on the bench. Turns the sign face out. Blank. Holds it up with everyone else.

The woman reads on.
